---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  SITE_NAME,
  SITE_DESCRIPTION,
  BOOK_SUBTITLE,
  BOOK_EDITION,
  PDF_PATH,
  LINKS,
  SITE_OG_IMAGE,
} from '../site';
---
<BaseLayout
  title={`${SITE_NAME} — Agentic engineering`}
  description={SITE_DESCRIPTION}
  canonicalPath="/"
  ogImage={SITE_OG_IMAGE}
>
  <section class="hero-site">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="edition">Garry Meaburn</p>
      <h1>
        Programming
        <span class="loop">the Loop</span>
      </h1>
      <p class="subtitle">
        Agentic engineering: working with coding agents, and measuring what helps instead of guessing.
      </p>
      <p class="byline">
        Free ebook, a worked example, and room for notes later. If something here is wrong, say so.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href="/book/">The book</a>
        <a class="button" href={LINKS.workedExample}>Worked example</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="what">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>What I write about here</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Coding agents can be capable when they have the right context, and expensive when you feed
          them everything “just in case”. A lot of advice assumes that the more you tell an agent
          about your repository, the better it does. I wanted numbers from controlled trials, not
          single-run anecdotes.
        </p>
        <p>
          This site is for that kind of work: practices, measurements, experiments and notes. Most of
          what is published so far is in the free ebook. Notes and separate experiment pages will
          appear when there is something worth putting up.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="book">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>The book</h2>
      <div class="feature-book">
        <div class="feature-book-visual">
          <a href="/book/">
            <img
              src="/book-cover.png"
              width="1600"
              height="2400"
              alt="Cover of Programming the Loop: What I Measured with Coding Agents"
            />
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="feature-book-copy">
          <p class="edition">{BOOK_EDITION}</p>
          <h3 class="feature-title">Programming the Loop</h3>
          <p class="feature-sub">{BOOK_SUBTITLE}</p>
          <div class="prose">
            <p>
              Eleven chapters on context, verification and the harness around coding agents. The
              measured findings come from small trials on a handful of repositories, with the limits
              written next to the numbers. Recommendations are my judgement; where they go beyond
              what I measured, the book says so.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="actions">
            <a class="button primary" href={PDF_PATH}>Download the PDF</a>
            <a class="button" href="/book/">Book page</a>
            <a class="button" href="/contents/">Contents</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="elsewhere">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul class="link-list">
        <li>
          <a href={LINKS.workedExample}>Worked example repository</a>
          <span class="link-note">
            Add an API endpoint with a cross-repository dependency — a reproducible demonstration,
            not a recorded agent session
          </span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={LINKS.feedbackIssue}>Open an issue</a>
          <span class="link-note">Corrections and questions on the worked example repo</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={LINKS.x}>X · @Mav1972</a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={LINKS.youtube}>YouTube · Frugal AI Engineer</a>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
